@@ -1,4 +1,23 @@
-# PipeWire
+# PipeWire — Floss desktop fork
+
+This Linux fork uses **Floss as its only Bluetooth stack**. The normal build
+includes `pw-floss`, which sends PCM to Floss; Bluetooth encoding and transport
+remain in Floss. The former BlueZ SPA plugins, Bluetooth codec implementations,
+MIDI backend and related dependencies have been removed. ALSA, general PCM
+processing, RTP and the other non-Bluetooth backends remain available.
+
+The integration is unfinished and has not been compiled or tested. Its current
+A2DP bridge requires an explicitly verified S16LE stereo feeding format because
+Floss does not expose the active PCM format over D-Bus. There is no automatic
+Bluetooth startup or codec/profile parity yet. The new `--profile hfp` path
+publishes headset playback and microphone nodes using negotiated mono PCM from
+the Floss fork: CVSD at 8 kHz or mSBC at 16 kHz. Floss retains voice encoding,
+and HFP does not require guessing a PCM rate. Profile selection remains manual. Read
+[src/tools/pw-floss.md](src/tools/pw-floss.md) before attempting playback.
+
+Linux and libdbus are required; disabling D-Bus is an explicit configuration
+error. There is no Bluetooth backend switch or BlueZ fallback. These fork
+changes do not describe upstream PipeWire's supported Bluetooth behavior.
 
 [PipeWire](https://pipewire.org) is a server and user space API to
 deal with multimedia pipelines. This includes:
